Publish this repo as-is. Do NOT run it through Jekyll.

GitHub Pages runs Jekyll over a repo by default. This site has no Jekyll content
— it is a hand-written static PWA (index.html + js/ + css/ served verbatim), so
Jekyll can only cost build time and invent failure modes. On 2026-08-06 the Pages
build began failing ("Page build failed") on every push from v14.0.75 onward
while v14.0.74 stayed live, which silently stranded four commits: the Firestore
persistence fix, the payroll Type A/B merge, a dead-code sweep, and the fix for
"Create task not working". Everything looked shipped — git push succeeded, the
version bumped — but nothing reached anyone.

The presence of this file (its contents are irrelevant) tells Pages to skip
Jekyll and publish the tree directly.

If a deploy ever appears not to arrive again, check the build status rather than
assuming a caching problem:
  gh api repos/barroindustries/barroindustries.github.io/pages/builds \
    --jq '.[0:5][] | "\(.status) \(.created_at) \(.commit[0:7]) :: \(.error.message // "-")"'
And note the second half of that failure mode: even once a build succeeds, the
service worker installs the new version into `waiting` and js/app.js only sends
it SKIP_WAITING when the user is sitting on the LOGIN screen. With 10-day auth
persistence a signed-in user may never see that screen, so a successful deploy
can still take days to reach them.
